url: "https://huggingface.co/datasets/akagabi/weather-rescue-brazil"
abstract: >-
  Daily, dekadal and monthly meteorological observations from six stations,
  1851-1890, transcribed from printed nineteenth-century tables: the Annales de
  l'Observatoire Imperial de Rio de Janeiro (French), the Revista do
  Observatorio (Portuguese) and the Astronomical and Meteorological
  Observations made at the Radcliffe Observatory, Oxford (English). Variables
  include barometric pressure read seven times daily, temperature maxima,
  minima and means, relative humidity, vapour tension, cloudiness, wind
  direction and force, rainfall, evaporation, ozone and actinometry.

  Transcription is by a Qwen3.5-2B model with a LoRA adapter running offline on
  a laptop. Each printed layout is described by a JSON profile declaring its
  columns, units, physical ranges and the arithmetic the page asserts about
  itself, which is used to judge every row without a human; fifteen of the
  eighteen layouts needed no labelled rows at all.

  The Oxford tables carry external validation, which the Brazilian ones cannot:
  Oxford has published the same observations, digitised independently more than
  a century later, and of the cells this dataset calls usable 100% of the dry
  bulb and 96.7% of the rainfall agree with that series, against 44.4% of the
  rainfall cells it flags. No row has been verified line-by-line by a human;
  the 3,822-cell evaluation gold set has been, and is separate from the dataset.
keywords:
  - data rescue
  - historical climatology
  - Brazil
  - Oxford
  - nineteenth century
  - table recognition
  - document understanding
